¶ [Tdr.D. J. Blaikley obligingly furnished me ¶ the substance of the following remarks on ¶ and repeated his experiments before ¶ in May 1884. The ordinary form of the ¶ is not wholly cylindrical. It is slightly ¶ at the mouthpiece and provided ¶ a spreading bell at the other end. The ¶ of form by key and finger holes ¶ must not be neglected. On a cylindrical ¶ played with the lips, the evenly numbered ¶ are quite inaudible. When a clarinet ¶ was added I found traces of the ¶ and 6th partials beating with my forks. ¶ on the clarinet with the bell, the 2nd, ¶ and 6th partials were distinct, and I could ¶ beats from them with my forks. ]Mr. ¶ brought them out (1) by bead and ¶ resonators tuued to them (fig. 15, ¶ 42rt), which I also witnessed, (2) by an irre¶ tubular resonator sunk gra¶ in water, on which I also heard them.

(3) by beats with an harmonium with a constant blast, which I also heard. On the cylindrical tube all the unevenly numbered partials are in tune when played as primes of independent harmonic notes. On the clarinet only the 3rd partial, or 2nd proper tone, can be used as the prime of an independent harmonic tone. The 3rd, 4th, and 5th proper tones of the instrument, are sufficiently near in pitch to the 5th, 7th, and 9th partials of the fundamental tone for these latter to be greatly strengthened by resonance, but the agreement is not close enough to allow of the higher proper tones being used as the primes of independent harmonic compound tones. Hence practically only the 3rd harmonics, ¶ or Twelfths, are used on the clarinet. ¶ following table of the relative intensity of the ¶ partials of a B\) clarinet was given by Blaikley in the Proc. of the Mus. Assn. for ¶ 1877-8, p. 84

